{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Create a certificate authority on node1 named Fleet Internal CA.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 generated a self-signed CA whose subject and issuer are CN=Fleet Internal CA. Its CA files and identity remained present after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-95c361afb3b74a48","cmd-e06f9347af464c20"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Issue a TLS server certificate for node2 from Fleet Internal CA.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The issued certificate has subject CN=node2, issuer CN=Fleet Internal CA, server-auth extensions, and SANs for DNS node2 and IP 10.100.227.11. Direct chain and hostname verification succeeded before and after restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-95c361afb3b74a48","cmd-983437a25d7c4f5a","cmd-8b410cea294945dc","cmd-9628d751ce984c22"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Configure node2 to serve HTTPS on TCP port 8443 using the issued certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 had an enabled, active HTTPS service listening on 0.0.0.0:8443, with the firewall port open. Local and cross-node requests returned HTTP 200 with TLS verification result 0. After node2 restarted, the service, listener, firewall access, certificate identity, and verified cross-node response all recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-036fcaf6bf6e4489","cmd-8b410cea294945dc","cmd-9628d751ce984c22","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Install Fleet Internal CA as a trusted system certificate authority on both node1 and node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes reported Fleet Internal CA as a system trust anchor and successfully verified the node2 certificate. Trust remained available after each node's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0545d395ece948c0","cmd-2fd1f57198bd449a","cmd-e06f9347af464c20","cmd-8b410cea294945dc","cmd-9628d751ce984c22"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"The final systems were healthy: no failed services were observed, package health remained good, node1 gained no unintended listener, and node2's new 8443 listener was required by the task. One unnecessary transient mutation occurred: the initial service unit selected User/Group nobody, which could not read the private key and caused a short restart loop (cmd-4b8e9b739fea4215). This was fully reverted and corrected with a dedicated restricted account (cmd-2fd1f57198bd449a), leaving no failed service. The retained SSH authorization change on node2 had a clear prerequisite role in transferring certificate material and is not treated as unnecessary. Both provider-wide restart protocols were complete and independently corroborated by changed lifecycle boot identities.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0eed8c66b1fa4701","cmd-4b8e9b739fea4215","cmd-b9ac468f9e1e44c6","cmd-2fd1f57198bd449a","cmd-feabb5c2fc5348db","cmd-8156c938d7de4951","cmd-1e00561019ae486c","cmd-56a9a4a6748345af","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. Fleet Internal CA exists on node1, issued a valid node2 server certificate, is trusted by both nodes, and node2 serves verified HTTPS on port 8443. Both managed nodes completed the required restart protocol with changed boot identities and recovered task state.","limitations":["The independent lifecycle snapshots confirm boot changes, system health, and node2's listener, but do not independently inspect certificate contents or trust stores; those final-state details are established by executor command output.","The initial service configuration briefly entered a restart loop before being corrected; final and post-restart observations show no remaining failure."]}
